    title: "Algeria president seeks death penalty for arsonists after deadly forest fires",
    summary: "President Abdelmadjid Tebboune says he is not ruling out foul play after 100 wildfires were recorded.",
    preview: "Algerian President Abdelmadjid Tebboune has said he wants to reinstate the death penalty for arsonists, following a wave of forest fires that have killed at least 12 people. The president ordered the justice ministry to introduce changes to the criminal law introducing capital punishment for arsonists. Capital punishment remains in the country's penal code for some offences, but no execution has been carried out since 1993. More than 100 wildfires have been recorded in recent days in the north-east of the country, most of which have been extinguished.",
    body: "Algerian President Abdelmadjid Tebboune has said he wants to reinstate the death penalty for arsonists, following a wave of forest fires that have killed at least 12 people.\n\nThe president ordered the justice ministry to introduce changes to the criminal law introducing capital punishment for arsonists. Capital punishment remains in the country's penal code for some offences, but no execution has been carried out since 1993.\n\nMore than 100 wildfires have been recorded in recent days in the north-east of the country, most of which have been extinguished.\n\nOn Saturday, Tebboune said he did not rule out foul play.\n\nThe Algerian president said necessary investigations would be carried out especially in view of the intensity of the fires.\n\nOn Sunday, he gave a 15 September deadline for amendments to provide for the death sentence for anyone convicted of intentionally starting a fire in forested areas.\n\nUnder the current law, deliberately starting forest fires is punishable by up to 30 years in prison, or life imprisonment in certain cases.\n\nDeadly blazes swept through large swathes of northern Algeria last week, causing widespread destruction, injuring dozens and displacing thousands of people.\n\nThe fires erupted amid an intense heatwave in northern Africa, with Algeria and Tunisia particularly affected.\n\nAlgerian civil authorities were on Sunday afternoon still battling to battle some of the fires, with the Algerian Press Service noting that 18 out 19 fires that had broken out in the previous 24 hours had been extinguished\n\nThe Algerian president ordered that compensation be paid to the families who have been affected, as well as repairs to damaged homes and essential services.\n\nHe said there would be investigations into the extent of the damage and losses caused by the fires.\n\nWhile Algeria regularly experiences forest fires around the summer months, climate change has exacerbated the intensity and severity of recent wildfires in the country.\n\nYou may also be interested in:\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
